{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>Before the gray head you shall rise, and you shall honor the face of the elder.</b> It appears that הִדּוּר (honoring) is more than קִימָה (rising), for the הִדּוּר includes the קִימָה as well. And זִקְנָה (old age) is more important than שֵׂיבָה (gray hair), for one must consider what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, saw to interpret 'זָקֵן' — this is one who has acquired wisdom (Kiddushin 32b) — in a place where it could be explained according to its plain sense. Rather, it was difficult for our Rabbis, of blessed memory: behold, הִדּוּר is more important than קִימָה, and שֵׂיבָה ought to be honored more than זִקְנָה, for sixty is the age of זִקְנָה and seventy the age of שֵׂיבָה. Rather, it is certain that the reason for honoring שֵׂיבָה is on account of the wisdom found among them more than on account of their old age, as it is stated (Job 12:12): ״בִּישִׁישִׁים חָכְמָה וְאֹרֶךְ יָמִים תְּבוּנָה״. Therefore Isi ben Yehuda said that every שֵׂיבָה is included, even an ignoramus (Kiddushin 32b) — since wisdom is found in him more than in his youth, it is fitting to honor him on account of his wisdom. But in any case, 'זָקֵן' — this is one who has acquired wisdom and exerted himself in it, for an acquired thing indicates the exertion of the acquirer who labored to acquire it, and it is intellect acquired by a person — such a one is certainly more fitting to be honored than שֵׂיבָה, in which wisdom comes to a person on its own, for 'in many days they will speak wisdom.'" "And since we find concerning the honor of father and mother and of one's teacher, that the Holy One, blessed be He, equated their honor to His honor and their reverence to His reverence (Kiddushin 30b) — lest you say that just as the honor of שֵׂיבָה and זָקֵן is upon you, so is their reverence imposed upon you, therefore Scripture says: ״וְיָרֵאתָ מֵּאֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — specifically the reverence of your teacher is like the reverence of Heaven, for he is called 'אֱלֹהֶיךָ,' as is found (Exodus 7:1): ״רְאֵה נְתַתִּיךָ אֱלֹהִים לְפַרְעֹה״, and as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Pesachim 22b): ״אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ תִּירָא״ to include Torah scholars. And this speaks of the reverence of one's teacher specifically. And the fact that Scripture called him 'זָקֵן,' a compound word, is to inform us that after he acquired his perfection in few days, he is like a seventy-year-old."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:43.099766535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}